[A repost - Carl]

[paraphrase: Here is my analysis of a university's responsibility when
a staff member runs an on-line archive. I think such an archive can be
either as a university archive or as a private archive, but steps
should be taken so that there is no confusion about which it is.]

Newsgroups: comp.org.eff.talk,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Subject:  Re: Fanny Hill Rides Again! (was Re: Censor on Internet..Pt-1)
Message-ID: <CKnwMx.21s@cs.uiuc.edu>
Date: Thu, 3 Feb 1994 18:56:56 GMT

greg@bronze.ucs.indiana.edu (Gregory R. TRAVIS) writes:

[...]
>Through all this, it's not been made clear to me how the University's
>obligations vis-a-vis the Constitution translate into obligations on
>Mike.  That is, given that he's operating in a purely volunteer role.

I can't give a definite answer to this, but I think I can clarify the
issues. First, I'll list what (in my opinion) is inherently unclear
and then what is clear.

What's inherently unclear:

   * Could a state university lose a lawsuit for officially removing
    _Fanny Hill_ from the library shelves just because it offensive.

   No one knows. The _Island Tree_ decision was indecisive and the
   Court has changed. On the other hand, some members on that Court
   felt that a similar removal from a *high school library* was
   unconstitutional. It is reasonble to predict that they would
   dislike removals from a university library even more.

   * Is CICA a limited public forum or a nonpublic forum. If it is a
    limited public forum who has access? Anyone's whose material meets
    topic requirements? Just the CICA archivist?  Put another way. Is
    the CICA archive most like 1) the quad/green/center, university mail,
    and usenet-at-the-university? 2) The university library were trained
    professionals select material which material is most important and
    valuable. Or 3) is it like the student newspaper (in which the
    student editor has complete editorial control)? Or 4) like a
    university Public Relations newspaper, which speaks for the
    university administration.

   No one knows.

What is clear (to me):

  * If the CICA archive is library-like, it's selection policy should be
    changed so that it no longer violates the Library Bill of Rights.

  * If the CICA archive is the personal editorial property of its
    archivist (the way a student newspaper is the personal editorial
    property of the student editor), then other students, professors,
    and staff members should be given similar opportunities to create
    archives. If limited resources make it impossible to allow
    everyone to have their own archive, then some fair way should be
    established for selecting who gets to be an archivist. (Look to
    the student newspaper's editor-selection policy for guidance).
    Without this, the CICA archivist looks like a state agent.)  Also,
    if "personal editorial property" is the case, the selection policy
    should be changed to say so.

(Aside: Having clear editorial authority likely makes one immune from
lawsuits. It does not, however, make one immune from criticism for
"bad" editorial decisions.)

Some other questions:

>For instance, a professor at I.U. is given a fine office and furniture
>for his or her use.  That professor is then allowed to control access
>to the office and the content of materials in the office.  There is
>no question as to ownership of the office or the furniture - the
>state owns it.

To the extent that a professor's office is a forum for speech,
professors usually have contractual guarantees that they can speak for
themselves (from university resources like their office) without
institutional censorship.

[...]
>Here's a question for Carl Kadie, or perhaps counselor Goodwin:  What's
>the legal rational for binding state institutions, such as a university,
>to constitutional restraints?  I can imagine a state, or the university
>itself, adopting a "no censorship" law, but what's the Constitution
>got to do with it?  I realize it must have something to
>do with the Fourteenth Amendment and/or the receipt of federal
>funds but...

It's just the way the courts have interpreted the 1st + 14th
amendments.

Federal funding doesn't currently apply. Funding only applies when
there is a law such as "If you censor, you will lose your funding."
There is no such federal law, but such a law has been discussed.
Also, California has such a law.

>How far do those restraints go - for example:  Why ISN'T a professor,
>who obviously receives state and federal funds (in the form of a
>salary), not bound by the First Amendment (i.e. why is he or she
>allowed to choose a religion)?  I don't buy that they're allowed to do
>so on "private" time because even then they're expending public
>funds (again, their salary).  This is a serious question - I'm not trying to
>bait anyone.

Because in his choice of religion, he is not acting as a state agent.

>All that says is that you, me, or Mike cannot be punished for publishing
>Fanny Hill as private individuals.  It says NOTHING about you, me, or Mike
>being OBLIGATED to publish it as private OR public individuals.

... except when we act as state agents.

- Carl

