From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Newsgroups: alt.censorship,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,can.general,mcgill.general
Subject:  Re: McGill U. treating computer media differently than paper media
Date: 12 Feb 1994 16:30:15 -0500
Message-ID: <2jjhp7$kue@eff.org>

Summary: McGill's Legal Advisor suggests that banning material is a
way to prevent censorship rather than a way to impose censorship. I
disagree.


================================
>	McGill					MEMORANDUM

>FROM:	Raynald Mercille
>	Legal Advisor
[...]
>SUBJECT:	Removal of a USENET "newsgroup"

[...]
>If I understand correctly, the procedure to make available a
>newsgroup locally is as follows.  A separate file folder is created
>so that local users can be fed segregated news items on demand.  If
>no separate file folder is created, the newsgroup is left in a junk
>file folder: in such a case, users outside McGill can be fed the
>newsgroup through the junk file, while local users cannot be fed.

I'm pretty sure this is technically incorrect. Local users *can* read
"junk".  Also, "junk" need not be fed to the outside.

(Right?)

[...]
>By receiving a newsgroup and packaging it in a special folder for
>local users, the University is actually processing, editing and
>promulgating a newsgroup for local consumption, all of which is an
>activity which could be construed as publication.

A library doesn't just let newspapers sit on the loading dock. It
unpacks them, it catalogs them, it puts them on a shelve above a name
tag. Many academic libraries also microfilm some newspapers for
archival purposes and photocopy articles when such coping is allowed
as fair use. Much of this processing is done manually by University
staff.

In contrast, Usenet processing is usually completely automatic. What
copying there is an inherent part of the medium. For example, display
requires copying information from a computer disk to a computer's
random address memory (RAM). In the U.S., such copying computer
material from disk to RAM is not even considered copying for the
purposes of copyright. If Canadian law is similar, it is hard to see
how such copying could be considered publishing.

But suppose that McGill is a publisher of all information it receives
and "processes". Wouldn't it then liable for any libel in that
information? As a major university, McGill receives and processes all
types of information from all over the world. Is McGill really
assuming the liability of a publisher of all that information?

[...]
>If your suggested approach had resolved the legal risk, which I do
>not believe it did, I would have raised with you some very serious
>policy considerations.

>First, you have yourself in the past expressed an extreme
>sensitivity to what you perceived as censorship.  There is no
>censorship in Vice-Principal Tavenas' decision, because the
>decision was not an imposition by Vice-Principal Tavenas of his own
>values on others:  rather Vice-Principal Tavenas did what had to be
>done by law.

So if the legal risk can resolved (say with a more accurate
understanding of how "junk" files work), and Vice-Principle Tavenas
continues to ban the newsgroups, he would be censoring.
(If the legal risk cannot be resolved, then the law is censoring.)

>Censorship is a process of editing material to deprive another from
>access to it.  Vice-Principal Tavenas' decision deprived even the
>supposed censor from having materials to edit: it was a blackout for
>all.

This suggests that banning material is a way to prevent censorship
rather than a way to impose censorship. This suggestion is contrary to
my understanding of the word "censorship".

>The suggestion to allow McGill to access the banned material
>while preventing other institutions in Quebec from such benefit
>raises a real argument of censorship by McGill of this information
>which McGill can access, 
[...]

Consider the two scenarios:
  1) McGill bans the newsgroups.
         McGill students and staff can't read them.
         Other universities are on their own.
      Likely effect: No one at any Quebec university will have access.

  2) McGill subscribes to the newsgroups, but does not promulgate them.
         McGill students and staff can read them.
         Other universities, following McGill's example, will acquire
                the newsgroups on their own.
      Likely effect: Everyone one at any Quebec university will have access.

Course #2, not #1, is the one that avoids censorship.

[...]
>[T]he Gazette could have published an article tomorrow on "McGill
>surreptitiously avoids the spirit of the law", and we would be left
>with the cold comfort of having respected the letter of the law.  By
>pulling the plug on the newsgroup globally, although he was forced
>to, Vice-Principal Tavenas ironically preserved the very freedom you
>now enjoy.  By contrast, an unbridled approach or a cunning evasion
>could possibly hasten the elimination of that freedom.
[...]

This suggests that the best way to defend freedom of expression is to
avoid using it, even when such use is legal. I disagree. I think
self-censorship is a bad strategy for institutions of higher
education. In any case, it should really be the University Senate that
decides which strategies, among the legal strategies, best reflect the
mission and values of the University.

